athcal O} P_a(o)U(o),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(P_a\\) is the distribution used to evaluate the hypothetical choice of \\(a\\). The dispute is about how to construct \\(P_a\\).\n\n**Evidential decision theory (EDT)** uses ordinary conditioning:\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{EDT}(P,x)\n\\in\\arg\\max_a\n\\mathbb E_P[U\\mid X=x,A=a].\n\\]\n\nIt asks what choosing \\(a\\) would be evidence of, given observation \\(x\\). In the paper’s formulation, actions assigned probability zero cannot be evaluated this way.\n\n**Causal decision theory (CDT)** instead uses a causal intervention:\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{CDT}(P,G,x)\n\\in\\arg\\max_a\n\\mathbb E_P[U\\mid \\operatorname{do}(A=a),X=x].\n\\]\n\nInformally, it changes the physical action and propagates changes through its causal effects, while holding fixed variables that the action does not cause.\n\nA-FDT also uses an intervention, but on a different variable. It changes the output of the decision computation and propagates changes through the dependencies attributed to that output. These include ordinary causal effects, but can also include a predictor’s earlier prediction or another implementation of the computation.\n\nThus A-FDT is not simply a compromise between EDT and CDT. It makes a different proposal about which hypothetical changes are relevant.\n\n## 2. The A-FDT Equation\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the agent’s probabilistic model, \\(G\\) a graph describing relevant dependencies, and \\(x\\) the observation history. Within the model, define a variable\n\n\\[\nD_x=\\operatorname{afdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x),\n\\]\n\nrepresenting the output of the very A-FDT procedure being evaluated on those inputs. The underlining indicates that the inputs identify the modeled computation; \\(D_x\\) is a *variable representing* its output, not the output itself.\n\nEquation (4), expressed with the assigned name, is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\operatorname{A\\!-\\!FDT}(P,G,x)\n\\in\n\\arg\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\n\\mathbb E_P\\!\\left[\n U\\mid \\operatorname{do}(D_x=a)\n\\right].\n}\n\\]\n\nFor each candidate \\(a\\), the model is surgically modified so that \\(D_x=a\\). The downstream variables are then recomputed according to \\(G\\), and the resulting expected utility is calculated. A maximizing action is returned; a complete implementation also needs a tie-breaking rule.\n\nThe intervention is on a **type of computation**, not merely on the particular bodily act that implements it. If the model says a predictor’s simulation and the agent’s action both depend on \\(D_x\\), both can change in the hypothetical. If it says a disease has no such dependency, the disease need not change.\n\nThere is a striking difference from the displayed CDT equation. Equation (4) does **not** add a separate condition \\(X=x\\) to the expectation. The observation determines *which decision-output variable* is intervened on. In a hypothetical where the output of that variable changes, the observation actually encountered in the modeled scenario may change too.\n\nSection 6 contains a displayed transparent-box expression that appears to put an observation inside the intervention, but its accompanying calculation and explanation allow the imagined observation to change. Taking Equation (4) as primary, as we do here, gives that latter interpretation.\n\nThe self-reference is intentional: A-FDT identifies a variable representing its own output. The paper observes that a program can represent itself without first knowing what it will output. That addresses a representational concern, not the harder question of how to construct a correct graph of logical dependencies.\n\n## 3. Subjunctive Dependence and Counterpossibilities\n\nThe paper calls the relevant relationship **subjunctive dependence**. Two physical calculators can compute the same function without communicating. If both work correctly on the same input, their outputs agree because of what they compute, not because one output causes the other.\n\nSimilarly, a predictor may simulate an agent’s decision process before the agent acts. In a hypothetical where the output of that process differs, the simulation’s result may differ as well. This is not a claim that the agent sends a signal backward in time. It is a proposal about how to construct a decision hypothetical while preserving a modeled computational relationship.\n\nMere correlation is not enough. If a lesion causes both a desire to smoke and cancer, that does not by itself make cancer depend on the output of a decision computation. A-FDT aims to preserve dependencies of the right kind and sever correlations that arise only from common causes.\n\nThe difficulty is that the hypothetical can be **counterpossible**. If a fixed deterministic computation on fixed inputs actually outputs \\(a_1\\), then “that computation outputs \\(a_2\\)” is logically false. Ordinary conditioning on that proposition is not generally available, especially if it has probability zero. Nor does classical logic alone tell us which consequences to retain while supposing it true.\n\nThe paper therefore treats \\(G\\) as supplied input: it specifies how the relevant variables would respond to the logical intervention. This is an idealization, not a general method for discovering logical structure. A graph that says the predictor tracks the decision output builds in an important premise; the A-FDT equation does not establish that premise by itself.\n\n## 4. Newcomb’s Problem Under A-FDT\n\nA transparent box contains \\$1,000. An opaque box contains either \\$1,000,000 or nothing. Earlier, a predictor filled the opaque box if it predicted that the agent would take only that box. Assume the predictor is accurate with probability \\(0.99\\), with the same error rate for either decision output.\n\nLet \\(D\\) represent the relevant A-FDT output. A simplified model has\n\n\\[\nD\\longrightarrow A,\\qquad\nD\\longrightarrow \\text{Prediction}\\longrightarrow \\text{Box contents},\n\\]\n\nwith a noise variable controlling predictive error. The arrows from \\(D\\) encode computational dependence, not physical causation from the present decision to the past.\n\nUnder intervention on \\(D\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{EU}(\\text{one-box})\n &=0.99(\\$1{,}000{,}000)\n   =\\$990{,}000,\\\\\n\\operatorname{EU}(\\text{two-box})\n &=0.01(\\$1{,}000{,}000)+\\$1{,}000\n   =\\$11{,}000.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA-FDT one-boxes.\n\nCDT, by contrast, holds the already-filled or already-empty opaque box fixed under intervention on the physical action. Taking the transparent box then adds \\$1,000 either way. EDT ordinarily one-boxes too, but because taking one box is evidence of a favorable prediction.\n\nThe numerical conclusion depends on the predictor model. More generally, if \\(p_1\\) is the probability of a full box under the one-box output and \\(p_2\\) its probability under the two-box output, one-boxing is preferred precisely when\n\n\\[\n\\$1{,}000{,}000(p_1-p_2)>\\$1{,}000.\n\\]\n\nA predictor’s overall success rate across a population does not automatically establish these two intervention probabilities for this agent. Predictive errors may depend on the action, the procedure, or details of the situation. Those are modeling questions.\n\n## 5. Why A-FDT Does Not Manage Every Correlation\n\nIn the imaginary smoking-lesion problem, a lesion causes both a taste for smoking and a high probability of cancer. Smoking itself does not cause cancer, and smoking gives the agent an additional \\$1,000 in utility.\n\nA simple A-FDT model in which the decision-output intervention changes smoking but not cancer gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{EU}(\\text{smoke})\n &= \\Pr(\\text{survive})\\cdot \\$1{,}000{,}000+\\$1{,}000,\\\\\n\\operatorname{EU}(\\text{refrain})\n &= \\Pr(\\text{survive})\\cdot \\$1{,}000{,}000.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA-FDT smokes. A naive EDT calculation may instead refrain if observing smoking would be strong evidence of the lesion.\n\nThe paper’s more careful model complicates the example: the lesion can determine which preference-dependent decision procedure controls behavior. Intervening on one procedure’s output changes the action only in the cases where that procedure is in control. The smoking advantage is then weighted by the probability of those cases. The lesion and cancer remain unaffected by the intervention.\n\nThe paper also acknowledges that the case is contrived. In particular, an EDT agent with sufficient self-knowledge may remove the relevant evidential correlation; the authors discuss “tickle” and ratification defenses and ultimately recommend abandoning the example as a test. Its useful lesson is narrower: **a statistical association between action and outcome is not itself a reason for A-FDT to vary the outcome when varying the decision output**.\n\n## 6. Observations That Change in the Hypothetical\n\nNow make both Newcomb boxes transparent. The predictor fills the large box if it predicts that the agent would one-box *upon seeing it full*. Suppose the agent actually sees it full.\n\nWrite \\(D_F\\) for the decision output on seeing a full box and \\(D_E\\) for the output on seeing an empty box. Assume the predictor predicts \\(D_F\\) correctly with probability \\(0.99\\). If the box is empty, taking both boxes is worth \\$1,000; let \\(q\\) be the probability that \\(D_E\\) says to take both.\n\nUnder Equation (4), evaluating \\(D_F=\\text{one-box}\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{EU}_F(\\text{one-box})\n=\\$990{,}000+0.01q(\\$1{,}000).\n\\]\n\nWith probability \\(0.99\\), the predictor fills the box and \\(D_F\\) controls the action. With probability \\(0.01\\), the box is empty and \\(D_E\\), not \\(D_F\\), controls the action.\n\nEvaluating \\(D_F=\\text{two-box}\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{EU}_F(\\text{two-box})\n=0.01(\\$1{,}001{,}000)+0.99q(\\$1{,}000).\n\\]\n\nThe first value is larger for every \\(q\\in[0,1]\\), so A-FDT one-boxes upon seeing a full box. When the box is empty, intervening on \\(D_E\\) does not change the predictor’s forecast of \\(D_F\\); taking both boxes then adds \\$1,000. Thus the modeled behavior is one-box if full, two-box if empty.\n\nThis is a counterintuitive result. In the hypothetical where \\(D_F\\) outputs two-box, the agent will probably see an *empty* box, even though the actual observation is a full one. A-FDT is not claiming to have learned that the box is empty. Rather, it evaluates the output of the full-box decision procedure across a model in which the circumstances under which that procedure is called can themselves depend on its output.\n\nThe same structure explains the paper’s treatment of **Parfit’s hitchhiker**. A driver rescues a stranded agent only if she predicts that the agent will pay after reaching town. The relevant decision output is the answer to “pay upon arriving?” A-FDT can pay in town because, in the modeled alternative where that output is “refuse,” the driver likely would not have offered the ride. Again, the model’s prediction accuracy and the driver’s decision rule are substantive assumptions.\n\n## 7. A-FDT, Threats, and Error Rates\n\nConsider a mechanical blackmailer who runs a damaging program only if she predicts the recipient will pay \\$1,000 upon receiving a demand. If run and not stopped by payment, it causes \\$1,000,000 of damage. The recipient has received the demand.\n\nUnder a perfect predictor, the output “refuse when demanded” makes the demand absent in the A-FDT hypothetical. The output “pay when demanded” makes it present and costs \\$1,000. A-FDT therefore refuses, even though paying would stop an already-running program in the actual situation.\n\nImperfection matters. As a concrete additional model, suppose a paying agent is always targeted, while a refusing agent is mistakenly targeted with probability \\(\\varepsilon\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{EU}(\\text{pay})=-\\$1{,}000,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{EU}(\\text{refuse})=-\\varepsilon \\$1{,}000{,}000.\n\\]\n\nRefusal is preferred if \\(\\varepsilon<0.001\\); payment is preferred if \\(\\varepsilon>0.001\\). This is not a universal rule to resist threats. It follows from the assumed targeting mechanism, costs, and intervention probabilities. If the threat is issued regardless of the agent’s decision process, refusal may simply incur the damage.\n\nThe example also shows why actual evidence and decision hypotheticals should not be confused. Receiving a demand may be strong evidence that a predictor has made an error if the agent will refuse. But Equation (4) evaluates the decision-output variable without additionally fixing receipt of the demand in every hypothetical. Whether that is normatively compelling is part of the dispute, not a theorem of probability.\n\n## 8. Applying A-FDT to Unfamiliar Cases\n\nA practical use of A-FDT begins by making the dependency assumptions explicit:\n\n1. **Specify the decision output.** Identify the modeled computation \\(D_x\\), its inputs, available actions, and utility function. Distinguish it from the physical action \\(A\\).\n2. **Model the environment.** Include predictors, other agents, observations, physical consequences, and relevant sources of uncertainty.\n3. **Specify responses to interventions.** For each candidate output, say which predictions, actions, and observations change, and which background variables remain fixed.\n4. **Compute expected utilities.** Apply the intervention to \\(D_x\\), propagate the model, and average utility over the resulting outcomes.\n5. **Test sensitivity.** Vary predictive accuracy, costs, and dependency assumptions. A recommendation that reverses under small changes requires more evidence about the model.\n\nFor example, in a prisoner’s dilemma against an exact computational twin on the same input, a model can connect both actions to \\(D\\). If mutual cooperation pays each \\$1,000,000 and mutual defection \\$1,000, A-FDT cooperates. Against an unrelated opponent whose action does not depend on \\(D\\), the same model does not justify assuming cooperation; defection may be better.\n\nFor imperfectly similar agents, my suggested operational approach is to assign explicit response probabilities such as\n\n\\[\nr_C=\\Pr(B=C\\mid\\operatorname{do}(D=C)),\\qquad\nr_D=\\Pr(B=C\\mid\\operatorname{do}(D=D)),\n\\]\n\nthen use the actual payoff matrix to compare the two expectations. This is a modeling proposal, not a probability rule supplied by the paper. It forces the important question into view: how much would the other agent’s behavior change under the relevant computational intervention?\n\nThe same caution applies to randomization. A shared pseudorandom seed that a predictor can reconstruct, an independent private coin, and a predictor that can inspect the coin are different models. Equation (4) alone does not say which source of randomness is shared or predictable.\n\n## 9. Actions, Policies, and Open Questions\n\nAn action is an output for one input; a policy \\(\\pi\\) maps possible observations to actions. The paper notes that its authors prefer a formulation that selects policies, and that this can matter in multi-agent problems. Equation (4), however, intervenes on \\(D_x\\), the output associated with a particular observation history. One should not silently replace it with optimization over all mappings \\(\\pi:X\\to\\mathcal A\\).\n\nAs an additional operational extension, one could introduce a variable \\(\\Pi\\), evaluate\n\n\\[\n\\pi^*\\in\\arg\\max_\\pi\n\\mathbb E[U\\mid\\operatorname{do}(\\Pi=\\pi)],\n\\qquad\\text{then execute }\\pi^*(x).\n\\]\n\nThis resembles policy selection, but it requires a model of how predictors and other agents respond to a whole policy. It may coordinate choices at different observations differently from separate interventions on \\(D_x\\). The paper’s note does not settle all such cases.\n\nSeveral further questions remain open:\n\n- **Graph construction.** How can an agent infer computational dependencies rather than merely statistical correlations? The paper identifies this as a principal unsolved problem.\n- **Counterpossible consistency.** Which mathematical relationships should survive an intervention on a false logical output? Different graphs can give different answers.\n- **Information and timing.** Equation (4) makes observations select intervention points, but does not provide a general recipe for separating information that should be held fixed from information that may vary with the decision output.\n- **Tractability and self-reference.** Exact modeling and optimization may be infeasible; representing one’s own algorithm does not by itself solve prediction or counterfactual reasoning.\n- **Performance claims.** The authors argue that their approach performs well in “fair” dilemmas, where outcomes depend on behavior in the problem rather than directly rewarding a decision theory. They also acknowledge that fairness lacks a complete formal definition and that no theory wins in every constructed problem. General optimality is therefore unproved.\n\nThe appeal of A-FDT is partly that it can act as an agent would have wanted to precommit to act before a prediction was made. But that resemblance is not an independent proof that every A-FDT action is optimal, nor does Equation (4) alone establish that information always has non-negative value. Such results need assumptions about available policies, models, and how information affects them.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nA-FDT evaluates actions by intervening on the output of the decision procedure rather than on the physical act alone. Its distinctive counterfactuals can preserve links to predictors and computational counterparts while rejecting merely statistical correlations. This yields one-boxing in the modeled Newcomb problem, smoking in the lesion model, and sometimes cooperation or resistance to threats.\n\nIts central limitation is equally clear: the equation works only with a model that says what depends on the decision output. Building and justifying that model—especially for imperfect predictors and logically impossible alternatives—is the main work left to an application.\n\n**Reference:** Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares, *Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality*, especially Sections 3–6 and Equation (4). [arXiv:1710.05060v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.05060v2).","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Drafting observations**\n\nI need to aim for about 2400 words for a specific section while ensuring I note any discrepancies. It looks like there's a typo to correct in section 6, so I’ll keep an eye on that. Also, I need to include a note about a blackmail error threshold correction with a source of 0.011, and yes, I’ll confirm payment details. Overall, I'm looking at writing between 2300 and 2500 words.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Drafting observations**\n\nI need to aim for about 2400 words for a specific section while ensuring I note any discrepancies. It looks like there's a typo to correct in section 6, so I’ll keep an eye on that. Also, I need to include a note about a blackmail error threshold correction with a source of 0.011, and yes, I’ll confirm payment details. Overall, I'm looking at writing between 2300 and 2500 words.","format":"openai-responses-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"gAAAAABq8PqRa-mXAwr7Fy16vbP_tLPAuyiy4ynhkJ0Ay3ADs5uN-2P_cPTvaIYyAoWJ_cXJN4IRxQdz-H5tpqWx4YjbjlidCzV8ykkuPSFr0cM47fLx-BZZ8Ux9nGf1zXhDWQpD4AiZWSljq6i-TbKbYfqOg-g1lp162TEE7IkOSkLLvCCAf4Zg-oFGa_jNgJNXAuY1e7Bk5Sa4o7mAI2CKnluF90tRNyLC1x0wLKZwheXWnNIPuMBgiLTc-D9ck-X09H3hj095dDVSA7wnEp-zxaT1yMpcgM71m29sp-cZGkTtWtyKG81fIxpc5DnWo2H2xMgBw8-dHsjS1okUyKoDSfuZ-6YrwViLuBl02s0AlRqc8u9DThHGEfGbMGfJEzMzY9CcyAnGasmPR3A5vRGL9oBf7McSIH4gdRHkyeTgaKMIc_oFMPPDZ_lVpFagoL9dCWKDMyUtb0aG5pxDWB17wP2Y3wp96D_6W8XNQYUBJDt0igXe5NWYrIiSwshGgAV6TZ9Lx5f1K2lRv66kHw0uIu42_2cqmcIvdg37OLrWJm2Xu0G_-42zYZZutFPxMMPfRyF0HPTdp3hfSULR3pOVxw8z66DPW